": "Next is one of my favourite facts from this quiz", "role": "elaboration", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-03-29T16:09:08Z", "username": "Chinmay Mehta", "text": "9. In the late 1960s, a city council in England put a historic crossing up for auction. It was a plain, practical Victorian structure — five granite arches, entirely functional, entirely unremarkable to look at. An American entrepreneur purchased it for over two million dollars, reportedly labouring under the impression that he was acquiring a far more ornate and photographed structure located a short distance downstream. Undeterred upon discovering the truth — or perhaps never fully corrected — he had the bridge dismantled stone by stone, each block numbered, shipped across the Atlantic, and painstakingly rebuilt over a man-made waterway in the American desert southwest. It stands there today as the centrepiece of a purpose-built lakeside town. The more famous bridge, the one he likely wanted, was never for sale.\nId X (the bridge that was actually sold), Y (the bridge it was famously confused with), and Z (the city and US state where it stands today)", "role": "elaboration", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-03-29T16:09:25Z", "username": "Pavan Pamidimarri", "text": "london bridge", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-03-29T16:09:28Z", "username": "Pavan Pamidimarri", "text": "lake havasu city", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-03-29T16:09:29Z", "username": "Pavan Pamidimarri", "text": "az", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-03-29T16:09:30Z", "username": "Pavan Pamidimarri", "text": "arizona", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-03-29T16:09:31Z", "username": "Srijan Naayak", "text": "Tower br